**Measured.** The consecutive-prime wheel showed no material advantage at equal bit length and equal sign count. At 168 bits, the mean exponent \(\log H/\log W\) of the exact shortest rational representative was **0.1093 for the primorial and 0.1097 for the matched random wheel**. The paired 95% interval for the difference was \([-0.0023,0.0017]\). Both methods that found these representatives enumerated signatures. The lattice constructor produced substantially larger outputs. [Exact results](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/wheelgap_c/exact_summary.csv>)

## W1. Reconstructing the factoring problem

**Known — conductor interpretation.** Put \(W=\prod_{3\le\ell\le y}\ell\), over primes, and write \(\sigma_W(u)=((u/\ell))_{\ell\mid W}\) for units. The closest published integer formulation uses \(N=r^2p\). The public vector is \(\sigma_W(N)=\sigma_W(p)\). Equivalently, the Jacobi character \(x\mapsto(x/N)\) is induced by the character of conductor \(p\). For squarefree \(N=pq\), the public vector is the coordinatewise product of the two factor vectors. Supplying \(\sigma_W(p)\) separately would supply hidden-factor information. [Boneh, §3](https://www.cryptrec.go.jp/exreport/cryptrec-ex-1024-2001.pdf) · [Corrigan-Gibbs and Wu, discussion of Adleman–McCurley](https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~dwu4/papers/Jacobi.pdf)

**Proved — recovery height.** Let \(L_y\) be the least nonsquare \(1\bmod8\) with all odd-wheel signs positive. Require the positive integer \(h\) to match \(p\) both on the wheel and modulo 8. If \(ph<L_y\), then \(ph\) is a square, so \(h=ps^2\). Thus \(\gcd(h,N)\) splits \(N\) whenever \(h<N\). For a reduced positive rational \(a/b\), its sign vector equals \(\sigma_W(ab)\). The corresponding certificate is \(pab<L_y\), with \(pab\equiv1\bmod8\) and \(ab<N\). A bound on \(\max(a,b)\) must therefore be squared when used in this certificate. [Lukes–Patterson–Williams](https://cr.yp.to/bib/1996/lukes.pdf)

**Known and measured — the 22-bit clue.** \(131\#/2\) is 168 bits and has 31 signs. The published \(L_{131}=2{,}871{,}842{,}842{,}801\) gives \(\log_2\sqrt{L_{131}}=20.69\), or a target-specific limit \(\log_2 h<41.385-\log_2 p\). An exhaustive check gives a sharper finite interpretation. Among odd squarefree units, the first collision when matching modulo 8 occurs at **2,821,283 and 3,617,267**, both 22-bit integers. With odd-prime signs alone, the primes **1,910,369 and 2,262,629** already collide. Thus “roughly 22 bits” fits a *planted recovery range*. For uniform signs, at most \(2^{22}/2^{31}=1/512\) of targets can have an integer representative through \(2^{22}\). [Published table](https://cr.yp.to/bib/1996/lukes.pdf) · [Collision check](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/wheelgap_c/collision_check.py>)

**Proved — a rational reconstruction that applies to balanced semiprimes.** Given \(N=pq\), prescribe \(\sigma_W(N)\) and require \(\max(a,b)\le H<N\), with \(H\ge\max(p,q)\). The planted witness is \(p/q\). If the wheel rejects every nonsquare \(Nab\) in this box, any match has \(Nab\) square. Then \(p,q\mid ab\), and neither coordinate can contain both. A gcd factors \(N\). Conversely, factoring supplies the witness on these \(N\)-labelled instances. This explains why the rational height convention matters.

**Proved — a precise unconditional random-wheel version.** Take \(k=\lceil4\log_2N\rceil\) independent uniform primes in \([X,2X]\), with \(\log X=(\log N)^3\). For each nonsquare \(D=Nab<N^3\), its quadratic character has conductor at most \(4N^3\). The uniform prime number theorem with its exceptional-zero term gives \(\Pr[(D/\ell)=1]\le 1/2+o(1)\): the exceptional term has negative sign in the character sum. A union bound over fewer than \(N^2\) pairs gives failure probability \(N^{-2+o(1)}\). Wheel generation has polynomial expected bit cost. Under GRH, the least-prime-outside-a-subgroup bound gives the same rejection guarantee for consecutive primes through \(C(\log N)^2\). These reductions concern planted inputs. A factoring oracle for \(N\) gives no corresponding witness for an independently chosen sign vector. [Uniform PNT, Theorem 3.1](https://w3.impa.br/~goncalves/NotesDavenport.pdf) · [Lamzouri–Li–Soundararajan](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1309.3595)

**Speculative reconstruction.** The binary forms were plausibly \(Au^2+Bv^2\) and \(Au^2-Bv^2\), with \(AB=W\). At each wheel prime one term vanishes, leaving the opposite coefficient times a square when the surviving variable is a unit. For the indefinite form, a convergent to \(\sqrt{B/A}\) gives a residual of order \(\sqrt W\) divided by the next partial quotient. A 78–80-bit residual fits that scale. The fragment supplies no universal floor. The likely ideal setting is a quadratic order of discriminant \(D\). Genus characters specify a coset of \(\mathrm{Cl}(D)^2\) in the proper form class group. The exact \(D\) is unrecoverable from the brief. [Principal genus theorem](https://www.math.utoronto.ca/~ila/Cox-Primes_of_the_form_x2%2Bny2.pdf)

## W2. What the sieve literature gives

**Known.** Lehmer, Lehmer and Shanks explicitly computed prescribed sign patterns. Pseudosquares are the all-positive pattern with genuine squares excluded and a condition at 2. Hall and Lehmer’s primality application, the Lukes–Patterson–Williams sieve, and Sorenson’s parallel enumeration concern this special search. Peralta and Davenport study shifts at one prime, a different arrangement of the character constraints. [Prescribed quadratic character](https://oeis.org/A002189/a002189.pdf) · [Lehmer](https://cr.yp.to/bib/1954/lehmer.pdf) · [Sorenson](https://arxiv.org/abs/1001.3316) · [Peralta](https://cr.yp.to/bib/1992/peralta.pdf)

**Proved from published bounds.** For the *least* \(h\), an ascending scan costs \(h\,\mathrm{poly}(\log W)\). For consecutive primes, Pomerance–Shparlinski’s Graham–Ringrose argument guarantees a hit by \(\exp(3y/\log\log y)\) asymptotically for every pattern: inserting prescribed signs in their character expansion preserves every absolute error bound. For arbitrary odd squarefree wheels, Burgess gives the scan ceiling \(W^{1/4+\epsilon}\) for fixed \(\epsilon>0\) by the same expansion, absorbing divisor counts \(W^{o(1)}\). Under GRH, either wheel has a prime in every coset by \(O(4^k\log^2W)\). These are constructive upper bounds by scanning. I found no optimal runtime theorem for the minimum. [Pomerance–Shparlinski, §2](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/pseudonew2.pdf) · [Cubefree Burgess bound](https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.17778) · [GRH coset bound, Theorem 1.4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1309.3595)

**Known and proved — the difference-table coefficient.** Bernstein’s doubly focused enumeration takes \(H/M^{2+o(1)}\) for an average interval shift under his hypotheses, with \(M=\exp((\log2)\log H/\log\log H)\). At \(H=N^{1/4}\) its saving coefficient is \((\log2)/2\). The brief’s \((\log2)/4\) instead follows from materializing balanced half-wheel lists of size \(W^{1/2}2^{-k/2+o(k)}\) when \(W\asymp\sqrt N\) and \(k\sim\log W/\log\log W\). The same coefficient occurs in Hittmeir’s hyperbolic BSGS bound at search width \(\Delta\asymp\sqrt N\). A table-size calculation bounds that implementation’s improvement. [Bernstein, §§2–3](https://cr.yp.to/focus/focus-20030928.pdf) · [Hittmeir, Theorem 6.2](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.06427)

## W3. What reciprocity and lattice reduction can do

**Proved.** The proposed matrix on the wheel primes has zeros on its diagonal as **Legendre symbols**. A reduced numerator or denominator containing a wheel prime is a nonunit. With the full primorial, the only positive \(y\)-smooth unit is 1. Omitting 2 adds at most its one sign vector. For an external base \(q_i\nmid W\), binary linear algebra solves \(Ae=s\) whenever \(s\) is in the column span. Reciprocity applies to random primes too.

**Proved — a model covering LLL, BKZ and exact CVP.** For a fixed external base of \(m=k^{1+o(1)}\) primes and \(H=2^{O(k)}\), **either wheel can represent at most \(2^{o(k)}\) signs** at integer or rational height \(H\). Reduce the support of \(ab\le H^2\) modulo 2. Primes below \(\sqrt k\) contribute at most \(2^{\sqrt k}\) choices. At most \(O(k/\log k)\) larger primes fit, giving \(\sum_{j\le O(k/\log k)}\binom mj=2^{o(k)}\) choices. Thus even unlimited computation succeeds on at most \(2^{-k+o(k)}\) of uniform targets. The all-positive target gives the matching lower bound \(2^{-k}\) on achievable success. This comparison covers arbitrary basis reduction on that fixed base. Larger or target-selected bases remain open.

**Proved.** Scaling coordinate \(i\) by \(\sqrt{\log q_i}\) makes exact squared Euclidean distance in the affine parity lattice equal to the minimum \(\log h\): each parity has a minimizer with exponent 0 or 1. Babai’s exponential-in-dimension approximation gives insufficient height control. The repo’s Theorem J averages *single-integer Johnson certificates near \(kN\)*. Prop CV treats *squarefree smooth integers in that interval*. Neither gives a lower bound for unrestricted wheel decoding. The priced-probe \(1/3\) theorem requires conditional Dickman and quota hypotheses. [Babai guarantee](https://isa-afp.org/browser_info/current/AFP/Babai_Nearest_Plane/document.pdf) · [Theorem J](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/sihard_results.md:402) · [Prop CV](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/siconstruct_results.md:92) · [Model assumptions](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/lowerbound_results.md:25)

## W4. Experiment

**Measured.** Each size used 32 paired uniform targets. P is the odd primorial. R is a random replacement wheel matching both bit length and sign count. The table gives mean \(\log H/\log W\) with paired-trial bootstrap 95% intervals. A post hoc enumeration completed every censored minimum. [Preregistration](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/wheelgap_c/prereg.md>) · [Follow-up protocol](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/wheelgap_c/followup.md>)

| Bits / signs | Integer P | Integer R | Rational P | Rational R |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 42 / 11 | .298 [.279,.314] | .290 [.273,.304] | .184 [.177,.191] | .186 [.179,.193] |
| 82 / 18 | .236 [.228,.244] | .241 [.235,.247] | .141 [.137,.144] | .142 [.138,.146] |
| 120 / 24 | .216 [.211,.221] | .216 [.212,.220] | .124 [.121,.127] | .124 [.121,.126] |
| 168 / 31 | .197 [.193,.200] | .194 [.189,.198] | .109 [.108,.111] | .110 [.108,.112] |

**Measured.** At 168 bits the registered integer scan exhausted \(2^{22}\) in about 0.5 ms with zero hits for either wheel. `fpylll` LLL/Babai returned all 32 integers per wheel in 67/66 ms mean CPU, with exponents .473/.456. A signature table pairing \(\sigma(a)\) with \(\sigma(b)=s\,\sigma(a)\) found the exact rational minimum within \(2^{19}\) on 28/32 and 24/32 trials in 7.5/8.2 ms. The registered capped P-minus-R interval was \([-.0016,.0019]\), well above the advantage threshold of \(-.02\). Post hoc square-root weighting gave LLL integer exponents .480/.465. [Method timings and CIs](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/wheelgap_c/summary.csv>) · [Weight check](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/wheelgap_c/sqrt_weights.csv>)

**Measured limitation.** R shared 29.9 of 31 primes with P on average. The primorial minimizes the product at fixed rank, making exact joint matching restrictive. A separate 168-bit control with 16 random primes gave integer exponent .092 [.087,.098] and rational exponent .055 [.052,.057]. Matching bit length alone confounds wheel structure with the number of constraints. [Raw trials](</Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/wheelgap_c/trials.jsonl>)

## W5. Verdict

**Proved / open.** The fixed-base counting obstruction rules out the proposed uniform-target constructor at the stated scale. The factoring question that survives is whether public primorial signs of a balanced \(N=pq\) admit a decoder returning a factor-bearing rational of height \(O(\sqrt N)\), with inverse-polynomial success and total classical cost \(L_N[\alpha,O(1)]\) for some \(\alpha<1/3\). The random-target measurements establish neither success on that planted distribution nor an unrestricted impossibility theorem.

**Speculative.** My probability that this primorial-decoding direction yields a classical sub-\(L[1/3]\) algorithm for general balanced semiprimes in the next decade is **below 1%**. The missing step is constructing a witness that separates the factors. Exact local sign matching alone already costs little.